"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Preoperative PSA was elevated at 8.5. The surgical findings included a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2), pathological stage pT2c, positive surgical margins, and the absence of seminal vesicle invasion and lymphovascular invasion. A key uncertainty stems from the lack of documented nodal status, as no lymph nodes were removed, and missing nodal sampling widens this uncertainty. The difference between biopsy and prostatectomy findings is noted as the Gleason pattern and ISUP report are missing from the pathology report.\n\nThe primary driver for prognosis is the positive surgical margins and the T2c stage. Countervailing factors include the absence of extraprostatic extension and the lack of lymphovascular invasion. The prognosis is further complicated by the absence of previous notes, which prevents assessment of postoperative PSA or its trajectory. Information regarding postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory would significantly revise this prognosis. The current uncertainty in nodal status due to missing sampling also impacts the overall assessment. The fixed estimated horizon is 62.79 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 37.67–87.91 months; its coverage has not been validated."
